# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "A loop that adds two in each iteration".
id: adds-two
title: A loop that adds two in each iteration
topic: Loops
level: 2
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "A loop that adds two in each iteration"
tags: while, invariant, bounds, sequence

task:
  Find a loop invariant, and prove that the triple is valid using the rule
  for sequential composition, the assignment rule and the loop rule with
  consequence. Hint: the invariant has to relate the value of $s$ with the
  value of $i$.

  Then explain why the property $s = 2 * i$ alone is an invariant of this
  loop, but is not enough to prove the postcondition. Which condition is
  missing, and in which of the three premises of the loop rule is it
  needed? Choose the invariant above the steps: the proof succeeds with
  the first one and fails with the second one.

given:
  Loop condition B: $i < n$

pre: n >= 0
post: s = 2 * n

program:
  {P}
  {R4}
  i := 0;            // i1
  {R3}
  s := 0;            // i2
  {I}
  while i < n do {
    {I && i < n}
    {R2}
    i := i + 1;      // b1
    {R1}
    s := s + 2       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(i < n)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

choice: I: s = 2·i ∧ 0 ≤ i ≤ n
outcome: proves

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  At the loop head the values are $(i, s) = (0, 0), (1, 2), (2, 4), ...$,
  so $s = 2 * i$. The same formula comes from the postcondition $Q$, with
  $n$ replaced by $i$, the variable that counts the iterations. We also add
  the bounds of $i$: it starts at $0$ and the loop stops when it reaches
  $n$.
set: I = s = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n
text:
  The loop rule with consequence has three premises, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $i < n$: initialisation, preservation of $I$ by the
  body, and conclusion $I && !B ==> Q$.

step: R3, the weakest precondition of s := 0 for I
part: Initialisation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  The loop comes after two assignments, so initialisation is the triple
  $\{P\}$ `i := 0; s := 0` $\{I\}$. We calculate the weakest precondition
  of these two statements for $I$, from the last one. We substitute the
  assigned variable ($s$) with the assignment expression ($0$) in $I$.
calc:
  wp(s := 0, I)
  = (s = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n)[s <- 0]     // I
  = 0 = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n               // s is replaced by 0
  = i = 0 && 0 <= i <= n                   // 2 * i is 0 only when i is 0
  = i = 0 && 0 <= n                        // replace i by 0 in 0 <= i <= n
set: R3 = i = 0 && 0 <= n

step: R4, the weakest precondition of i := 0 for R3
part: Initialisation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($i$) with the assignment expression
  ($0$) in $R3$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(i := 0, R3)
  = (i = 0 && 0 <= n)[i <- 0]     // R3
  = 0 = 0 && 0 <= n               // i is replaced by 0
  = 0 <= n                        // 0 = 0 is true, and true && A is A
set: R4 = 0 <= n

step: P implies R4
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: P, R4
text:
  $R4$ is the weakest precondition of the two assignments for $I$. By the
  consequence rule, initialisation holds when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R4$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R4»
vc: n >= 0 ==> 0 <= n
why:
  The two formulas say the same thing.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of s := s + 2 for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant:
  >>> {«I» && «i < n : B»} i := i + 1; s := s + 2 {«I»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $s$ with $s + 2$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(s := s + 2, I)
  = (s = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n)[s <- s + 2]     // I
  = s + 2 = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n               // s is replaced by s + 2
set: R1 = s + 2 = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n

step: R2, the weakest precondition of i := i + 1 for R1
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$ in $R1$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, R1)
  = (s + 2 = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n)[i <- i + 1]     // R1
  = s + 2 = 2 * (i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n         // i is replaced by i + 1
  = s = 2 * i && 0 <= i + 1 <= n                   // 2 * (i + 1) is 2 * i + 2; subtract 2 from both sides
  = s = 2 * i && -1 <= i <= n - 1                  // subtract 1 from each part of the bounds
set: R2 = s = 2 * i && -1 <= i <= n - 1

step: I and B imply R2
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && i < n, R2
text:
  By the consequence rule, the triple holds when $I && B$ implies $R2$.
  >>> «I» && «i < n : B» ==> «R2»
vc: s = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n && i < n ==> s = 2 * i && -1 <= i <= n - 1
why:
  $s = 2 * i$ is part of $I$. From $0 <= i$ we get $-1 <= i$. For
  integers, $i < n$ is $i <= n - 1$.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && !(i < n), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and $B$ is false. This must imply the
  postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(i < n) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: s = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n && !(i < n) ==> s = 2 * n
why:
  $!(i < n)$ is $i >= n$. Together with $i <= n$ it gives $i = n$, and
  then $s = 2 * i$ becomes $s = 2 * n$.
result: The triple is valid, with the invariant $s = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n$.

choice: I: s = 2·i
outcome: fails

step: The candidate invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  At the loop head the values are $(i, s) = (0, 0), (1, 2), (2, 4), ...$,
  so $s = 2 * i$. We try this formula alone, without the bounds of $i$.
set: I = s = 2 * i
text:
  The loop rule with consequence has three premises, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $i < n$: initialisation, preservation of $I$ by the
  body, and conclusion $I && !B ==> Q$.

step: R3, the weakest precondition of s := 0 for I
part: Initialisation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  The loop comes after two assignments, so initialisation is the triple
  $\{P\}$ `i := 0; s := 0` $\{I\}$. We calculate the weakest precondition
  of these two statements for $I$, from the last one. We substitute the
  assigned variable ($s$) with the assignment expression ($0$) in $I$.
calc:
  wp(s := 0, I)
  = (s = 2 * i)[s <- 0]     // I
  = 0 = 2 * i               // s is replaced by 0
  = i = 0                   // 2 * i is 0 only when i is 0
set: R3 = i = 0

step: R4, the weakest precondition of i := 0 for R3
part: Initialisation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($i$) with the assignment expression
  ($0$) in $R3$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(i := 0, R3)
  = (i = 0)[i <- 0]     // R3
  = 0 = 0               // i is replaced by 0
  = true                // 0 = 0 is true
set: R4 = true

step: P implies R4
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: P, R4
text:
  By the consequence rule, initialisation holds when the precondition $P$
  implies $R4$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R4»
vc: n >= 0 ==> true
why:
  Every formula implies $true$.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of s := s + 2 for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  One iteration must keep the candidate:
  >>> {«I» && «i < n : B»} i := i + 1; s := s + 2 {«I»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $s$ with $s + 2$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(s := s + 2, I)
  = (s = 2 * i)[s <- s + 2]     // I
  = s + 2 = 2 * i               // s is replaced by s + 2
set: R1 = s + 2 = 2 * i

step: R2, the weakest precondition of i := i + 1 for R1
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$ in $R1$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, R1)
  = (s + 2 = 2 * i)[i <- i + 1]     // R1
  = s + 2 = 2 * (i + 1)             // i is replaced by i + 1
  = s = 2 * i                       // 2 * (i + 1) is 2 * i + 2; subtract 2 from both sides
set: R2 = s = 2 * i

step: I and B imply R2
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && i < n, R2
text:
  By the consequence rule, the triple holds when $I && B$ implies $R2$.
  >>> «I» && «i < n : B» ==> «R2»
vc: s = 2 * i && i < n ==> s = 2 * i
why:
  $R2$ is the candidate $I$ itself. So $s = 2 * i$ holds at the start and
  is kept by the body: it is an invariant of the loop.

step: I and not B do not imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && !(i < n), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and $B$ is false. This must imply the
  postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(i < n) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: s = 2 * i && !(i < n) ==> s = 2 * n
status: fails
counterexample: n = 1, i = 2, s = 4
why:
  $!(i < n)$ only gives $i >= n$. The state $n = 1$, $i = 2$, $s = 4$
  satisfies $s = 2 * i$ and $i >= n$, but $s != 2 * n$. The candidate is
  an invariant, but it is too weak: the bound $i <= n$ is missing. It is
  needed in the conclusion, where with $i >= n$ it gives $i = n$.
